# Codegraph Enhancement Prompt (v2)

Use this as the task prompt for your agent (Copilot Agent Mode / Codemind playbook) to
upgrade **codegraph**. Implement every feature below independently, in our own codebase
and coding style, using only standard/pip-installable Python libraries. Do not download,
clone, vendor, or wrap any external code-graph tool — only the described *behavior* is
in scope. **No LLM/gateway integration work is in scope for this task** — everything
below is deterministic, structural graph work (AST parsing, graph algorithms, file I/O).

This version is based on direct inspection of two months of real commit history and
source (not summarized secondhand) — every item below traces to an actual verified
fix or feature, not an assumption about what "probably" changed.

---

## 1. Graph integrity & quality hardening (highest priority)

This is the largest and most valuable category: making the graph *trustworthy*, not
just complete. A graph with plausible-looking but wrong edges is worse than a smaller,
correct one — false edges quietly corrupt every downstream query, blast-radius
analysis, and report.

**1.1 Cross-language contamination guard**
Calls/imports/references must never bind an identifier in one language to a
same-named symbol in a different, unrelated language family (e.g. a Python `import
time` resolving onto an unrelated `time.ts` file, or a JS callback binding to a
same-named Kotlin function). Add an explicit interop-family check at edge-resolution
time: an edge between two "known code" languages is only allowed when both sides
belong to the same interop family (e.g. JVM languages together, C-family together,
JS/TS module graph together). Config/manifest → code references are exempt. Without
this guard, a handful of coincidental name collisions can silently weld unrelated
subsystems together and manufacture a false top "hub" node that distorts every
centrality-based analysis.

**1.2 Builtin/stdlib type guard**
Member-call and type-reference resolution must never collapse onto a builtin/stdlib
type just because a user-defined symbol shares its name (e.g. a variable typed `Date`
binding to a user class literally named `Date` in another file). Maintain a
per-language builtin-type denylist checked before any receiver-type match is accepted.

**1.3 Unverified-node flagging**
Any node derived from a non-deterministic (model-assisted) pass that claims a code
symbol exists but that symbol cannot be found in the referenced source must be tagged
with an explicit `verification: "unverified"` field rather than presented as a
confirmed fact — and never silently dropped, just flagged for review. This check
compares the claimed label/id against the actual source content it's attributed to.

**1.4 Dedup precision**
Fuzzy/near-duplicate node merging needs several precision guards:
- Numbered or versioned siblings whose only difference is a digit run (e.g. two
  different ADR/RFC numbers, two different section numbers) must never merge just
  because the surrounding text is similar.
- Prose-derived nodes (documentation/rationale content) should be anchored to their
  source file the same way code nodes are — near-identical boilerplate across
  *different* files should not merge, while genuine duplicates *within* the same file
  still should.
- Use a stricter string-similarity metric (plain Jaro) for cross-file candidates and a
  looser one (Jaro-Winkler, which rewards shared prefixes) only for same-file
  candidates — a shared prefix bonus across files creates false merges when two
  distinct concepts happen to start the same way.
- When two records that share an exact ID from the same file are collapsed into one,
  preserve the union of both records' fields rather than discarding one — deduplication
  must never quietly lose data.

**1.5 Hyperedge integrity**
Multi-node relationship structures ("hyperedges" — one edge type connecting 3+ nodes
around a shared concept) need the same rigor as binary edges:
- Any hyperedge referencing a node that isn't actually present in the node set must be
  dropped before it reaches the persisted graph, not carried through as a dangling
  reference.
- Node/edge/hyperedge IDs must be coerced to a consistent type (string) at ingest —
  a non-string ID from any producer must not crash the build.
- A file whose only extracted output is a hyperedge (not a plain node/edge) still
  counts as "successfully processed" for caching/incremental-update purposes — don't
  let it get silently re-processed every run because the completion check only looked
  at nodes and edges.

**1.6 Self-loop / phantom-cycle elimination**
Import/reference resolution must actively reject edges whose source and target
resolve to the same node — these show up as fake "1-file import cycles" in reports and
usually come from an id-collision bug (e.g. a bare import alias resolving back onto the
importing file itself, or a same-basename file in two different extensions colliding
on a shared node-id stem). Add a build-time check: any `imports`/`imports_from`/
`references` edge with identical source and target endpoints is dropped, and existing
graphs should self-heal on next rebuild rather than requiring a manual fix.

**1.7 Deterministic traversal output**
Path-finding and shortest-path queries must return the *same* route on every run for
the same graph — not a route that varies because the underlying traversal iterates
over a non-deterministic structure. Sort/normalize the graph view before traversal.
Each reported hop must also show the edge's real stored relationship type, not
whatever relation happens to survive if multiple parallel edges between the same two
nodes were collapsed arbitrarily.

**1.8 Atomic writes for every persisted artifact**
Every file codegraph writes — the graph itself, manifest/cache files, any analysis or
label sidecars — must be written to a temporary file in the same directory and moved
into place with an atomic rename, never written in place. A crash, OOM kill, or
disk-full event mid-write must never leave a truncated file that then poisons every
subsequent run (a truncated graph file should be detected on load and rejected with a
clear recovery message, not silently trusted).

**1.9 Ranking-noise filtering**
Structural type annotations from a language's own type system (e.g. `str`, `int`,
`bool`, common mock/test-double types) must not themselves become graph nodes or
accumulate edges just because they appear as a parameter/return type — they inflate
degree-based "most connected" rankings with noise and crowd out real architectural
hubs. Filter these at extraction time, and add a defensive filter at ranking time too
so existing graphs benefit without a full rebuild.

**1.10 Pre-labeling graph-health gate**
Before running any expensive labeling/analysis pass, run a read-only diagnostic that
surfaces dangling edges, self-loops, and collapsed-parallel-edge risk as warnings. This
must never block or abort the pipeline — it's a visibility gate, not a hard failure.

