## 2. New/graduated tools to add

**2.1 First-class "most-connected nodes" command**
Expose the "which nodes are the biggest architectural hubs" capability as a direct,
documented top-level command (not just something buried inside a broader analysis
pass) — accepts a result-count limit and both human-readable and machine-readable
(JSON) output modes.

**2.2 Self-improving work-memory loop**
Add a deterministic (no LLM) feedback loop:
- A `save_result(question, answer, source_nodes, outcome=None, correction=None)` API
  where `outcome` is one of `useful` / `dead_end` / `corrected`, recorded per query.
- A `reflect()` pass that aggregates all recorded outcomes into a single lessons
  document, grouped by community/subsystem when a graph is available. Score each
  cited source node with a signed, time-decayed value (positive for `useful`, negative
  for `dead_end`/`corrected`, configurable half-life, e.g. 30 days by default) so a
  recent contradiction outweighs a stale success.
- Only promote a node to "preferred" once it's corroborated by multiple independent
  results (configurable minimum, e.g. 2) — a single citation should never be enough to
  mark something as trusted. Nodes with mixed positive/negative signal render as
  "contested," with the most recent signal winning the displayed verdict.
- Drop citations to nodes that no longer exist in the current graph so lessons don't
  reference stale/deleted code.
- This should be zero-config for the agent: the agent-facing entry point should read
  the lessons document automatically at the start of graph work, and the loop should
  be cheap enough to run as a no-op check when nothing has changed since the last run.

**2.3 Query/usage logging**
Log every query/path/explain-style call (timestamp, kind, question, corpus path, result
size, duration) to a local JSON-Lines log for observability and later analysis of what
agents actually ask the graph. Do not log full response bodies by default; make that
opt-in. Make the whole logging path itself optional and failure-safe — a logging error
must never block or fail the underlying query.

**2.4 Bounded response sizing on any graph-serving interface**
If codegraph exposes queries to agents through any RPC/tool-calling interface, cap
response size per call (e.g. a configurable token/character budget, sensible default)
so one call on a highly-connected node or large subsystem can't flood the caller's
context. Truncation must be clearly announced in the response (what was omitted, and a
hint for narrowing the query) — never silently truncated with no signal.

